    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000145045459": {
      "title": "PhD Scholarship: Settlement Agreements between First Peoples and Australian governments",
      "description": "We are currently looking for an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander PhD candidate to join our Australian Research Council (ARC) Discovery Project on Settlement Agreements between First Peoples and State governments in Victoria and Western Australia. The primary focus of this role is to develop a distinct PhD topic within the Discovery Project parameters that contributes new and innovative knowledge to the field of agreement-making between First Peoples and Australian Governments. The Project investigates how Settlement Agreements are negotiated and implemented and explores the various social, political and cultural dimensions that shape how they are made and managed.  Success in this role requires engagement with First Peoples groups and governments in Victoria or/and Western Australia, and collaboration with a small research team based at Griffith University and James Cook University. \n\n The selection of the applicant for the award of a Higher Degree Research Scholarship at Griffith University is based on academic merit and research background. Expressions of interest are welcome from Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander descent applicants, who identify themselves as such, and are accepted by the community with which they are associated with as an Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander. a Bachelor degree with second-class honours (Division A) or a Masters degree, incorporating a significant research component, from a recognised institution; or a record of research, or a qualification granted by a professional or other body deemed by the Dean, Griffith Graduate Research School, to be of a standard comparable to a Bachelor degree with second-class honours (Division A).  Refer to the  Equivalent Qualifications for HDR Program Admission  web page. \n\n About the scholarship \nThe 2026 Griffith University Indigenous Australian Postgraduate Research Scholarship has an annual stipend of $55,379 (indexed) for a period of up to four years of full-time study. Please see the  GUIAPRS Conditions of Award  for more information. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with a University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EoI process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to  ciaran.ofaircheallaigh@griffith.edu.au  or  bartholomew.stanford@jcu.edu.au  containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) using the  Griffith CV template . Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is: Friday 18 September 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line \n \n Further information For project related enquiries please contact: \nEmeritus Professor Ciaran O’Faircheallaigh \nPhone:  0419-023-741 \nEmail:   ciaran.ofaircheallaigh@griffith.edu.au Dr Bartholomew Stanford \nPhone: 0450-141-374 \nEmail:  bartholomew.stanford@jcu.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The School of Government and International Relations is among the highest-ranked politics schools or departments in Australia. It has received the top ranking of 5, ‘Well above world standard,’ in Political Science/International Relations in the past three Excellence in Research for Australia exercises, dating to 2012. The School consistently ranks among the very top Australian departments for research productivity and external funding, and it ranked first in Queensland in the most recent Shanghai rankings. Our research expertise is leveraged in extensive engagement activities with government and civil society, and in dynamic learning and teaching that has garnered numerous university and external awards The project is led by Emeritus Professor Ciaran O'Faircheallaigh and Dr Bartholomew Stanford. Ciaran has worked with First Peoples in Australia and internationally for over thirty years, and has written extensively about commercial agreements First Peoples have negotiated with mining, oil and gas companies. Bartholomew is a Torres Strait Islander political science researcher who studies Indigenous-state relations. This opportunity will be primarily based at Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-site University you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144659189": {
      "title": "Research Fellow / Senior Research Fellow",
      "description": "At Griffith’s School of Nursing and Midwifery , research drives real-world change. We challenge the status quo, partner with communities and industry, and translate evidence into practice to improve health outcomes for all. Our work spans innovation, policy and care delivery with a strong focus on equity and impact. We support emerging researchers, champion inclusive leadership, and collaborate globally to amplify our reach and results. We are seeking a Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow to join a dynamic multidisciplinary research program focused on improving prevention, acute care and recovery outcomes for children experiencing burns and trauma. The successful candidate will contribute to high-quality research spanning clinical studies, implementation science and health services research, working closely with clinicians, consumers, researchers and policy stakeholders to improve outcomes for children and families. Depending on experience, appointments may be made at either the Research Fellow or Senior Research Fellow level. As a Research Fellow, you will: Conduct and coordinate research activities that contribute to externally funded research programs focused on paediatric injury and burn care Design and undertake clinical, implementation and health services research projects, including clinical trials and cohort studies Develop and maintain collaborative relationships with clinicians, researchers, consumers and policy stakeholders Contribute to competitive research funding applications and develop a strong publication record Lead the preparation of publications, presentations, policy briefs and research reports Support co-design, stakeholder engagement, knowledge translation and implementation activities Manage research projects to achieve outcomes within agreed timelines, budgets and reporting requirements Assist with the supervision and mentoring of higher degree research candidates, research assistants and project staff. As a Senior Research Fellow, you will: Lead components of externally funded research programs in paediatric injury and burn care Design and oversee clinical trials, cohort studies and implementation research projects Drive interdisciplinary collaborations with clinicians, health services and policy partners Develop and lead competitive research grant applications, including NHMRC and MRFF funding schemes Contribute to strategic research development within the School of Nursing and Midwifery and Griffith Health Lead the production of high-impact publications, reports, policy briefs and conference presentations Drive stakeholder engagement, co-design and knowledge translation initiatives to maximise research impact Provide supervision and mentoring to higher degree research candidates, postdoctoral fellows and research staff Manage multi-site research projects and provide leadership within collaborative research teams. This is a fixed term (two year), full-time position primarily based in Brisbane at the Queensland Children's Hospital. As Griffith is a multi-site university, you may be required to work across other locations. Griffith University's sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range Research Fellow \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be $91,705 - $139,031 per annum plus 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $107,295 - $162,667 per annum. Senior Research Fellow \nThe full-time equivalent base salary will be $143,418 - $165,374 per annum plus 17% superannuation. The total FTE package will be in the range $167,799 - $193,487 per annum. \n\n To be successful in this role, you will bring a passion for improving child health outcomes through collaborative, high-quality research and a commitment to translating evidence into practice. You will also have: A PhD in nursing, public health, psychology, epidemiology, emergency medicine, or another relevant health discipline Demonstrated experience undertaking health or clinical research in a relevant field Strong stakeholder engagement skills and the ability to work effectively with clinicians, researchers, consumers and community partners Experience contributing to research publications and competitive funding applications Excellent communication, project management and organisational skills. In addition, Senior Research Fellow candidates will demonstrate: Significant postdoctoral research experience and a record of research leadership A strong track record of securing or contributing to competitive research funding Experience leading complex research projects and multidisciplinary collaborations Proven ability to supervise, mentor and develop researchers across career stages A sustained record of high-quality publications and research impact. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer: Salary packaging options and corporate health discounts Generous leave entitlements including paid parental leave and leave loading Opportunities for internal mobility How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Applicants must have unrestricted working rights. VISA sponsorship is not available for this position. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Bronwyn Griffin Deputy Head of School (Research & HDR) at bronwyn.griffin@griffith.edu.au For application and recruitment advice, please contact Maria Mahon, Talent Acquisition Partner at m.mahon@griffith.edu.au             Closing date:  Sunday 13th September at 11:59pm AEST.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": "School of Nursing and Midwifery"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144651369": {
      "title": "Research Fellow - Glycoproteomics in Cytokine Structure Biology",
      "description": "The Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at Griffith University embodies multidisciplinary expertise in glycoscience, biomedicine, structural biology and translational research. Our researchers use advanced technologies and collaborative approaches to understand the molecular basis of health and disease and translate fundamental discoveries into better health outcomes. We are seeking a highly motivated Research Fellow to join an exciting interdisciplinary project investigating how protein glycosylation regulates cytokine signalling during erythropoiesis and how this knowledge can be applied to improve the ex vivo production of red blood cells. The position is supported by the National Health and Medical Research Council Ideas Grant: Manufacturing Red Blood Cells: The Critical Role of Glycosylation in Cytokine Signalling. This $1.67 million collaborative project between Griffith University and Australian Red Cross Lifeblood will investigate how the glycosylation of key cytokines and their receptors influences erythroid-cell proliferation, differentiation, maturation and function. Red blood cell production is controlled by a complex network of cytokines and cell-surface receptors. Although many of these signalling proteins are extensively glycosylated, the ways in which their carbohydrate structures affect protein recognition, receptor activation and cellular outcomes remain poorly understood. The project will address this important knowledge gap and apply the resulting insights to the development of more efficient approaches for producing red blood cells outside the body. Improving ex vivo red blood cell production could contribute to future safe and reliable blood supplies, particularly for patients with rare blood types or complex transfusion requirements. The project brings together an interdisciplinary team with expertise in: Glycomics, proteomics and glycoproteomics Protein biochemistry and structural biology Cytokine and receptor signalling Ex vivo human erythropoiesis Red blood cell physiology and blood rheology The successful candidate will work with the research groups of Professor Daniel Kolarich, Dr Larissa Dirr and Professor Michael Simmonds at Griffith University, together with Dr Becky Griffiths at Australian Red Cross Lifeblood. This appointment offers an outstanding opportunity to develop an interdisciplinary research profile at the interface of glycoscience, structural biology, cell biology and transfusion research. Key responsibilities As the Research Fellow, you will: Design, conduct and analyse research investigating the role of protein glycosylation in cytokine signalling and erythroid development. Investigate selected cytokines and their receptors using complementary biochemical, glycomic, glycoproteomic, structural and cell-biological approaches. Contribute to experiments using ex vivo erythroid culture systems to determine how changes in glycosylation affect cell proliferation, differentiation, maturation and function. Work with project specialists to relate molecular and cellular changes to the functional, biophysical and rheological properties of developing and mature red blood cells. Develop, optimise and validate experimental protocols relevant to the project. Integrate and interpret results produced through multiple experimental platforms and participating laboratories. Apply rigorous approaches to experimental design, quantitative analysis, research reproducibility and data management. Prepare manuscripts for publication in high-quality peer-reviewed journals. Present research findings at project meetings and national and international scientific conferences. Coordinate research activities with investigators, research staff, students and external collaborators. Contribute to the supervision and development of honours and higher degree research students, where appropriate. Assist with project reporting, research governance, ethics, biosafety and laboratory compliance requirements. Contribute to the development of new grant applications and collaborative opportunities arising from the research. Participate actively in the research cultures of Griffith University, the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics and Australian Red Cross Lifeblood. About you \nYou will hold a PhD or equivalent qualification in biochemistry, molecular biology, glycobiology, proteomics, structural biology, cell biology, haematology or another discipline relevant to the project. You will have strong research experience in at least one of the project’s core areas and a genuine interest in learning and applying complementary approaches from the other participating disciplines. Applicants are not expected to already be experts in every area of the project. Specialist training and mentorship will be available through the interdisciplinary research team. To be successful in this position, you will demonstrate: Advanced research expertise in one or more of the following areas: Glycomics, glycoproteomics or mass-spectrometry-based proteomics Protein biochemistry, recombinant protein production or protein–protein interaction analysis Structural or biophysical characterisation of proteins Cytokine biology or cell-signalling research Mammalian or primary human cell culture Haematopoietic stem and progenitor cell biology or ex vivo erythropoiesis Red blood cell physiology, biomechanics or blood rheology The ability to design, conduct, troubleshoot and interpret complex experiments. A sound understanding of quantitative analysis, appropriate experimental controls and research reproducibility. A record of high-quality research outputs appropriate to career stage, including peer-reviewed publications. The ability to work independently while contributing effectively to a collaborative and multidisciplinary research program. A willingness and capacity to acquire new experimental and analytical skills across the project’s research areas. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate findings to researchers from different disciplinary backgrounds. Strong organisational and project-management skills, including the ability to manage competing priorities and deliver agreed milestones. A commitment to research integrity, laboratory safety, collegiality and an inclusive research culture. Experience in interdisciplinary research, collaborative project delivery or the supervision of research students would be advantageous. What we can offer \nThis is a three-year, fixed-term, full-time position primarily based within the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at Griffith University’s Gold Coast campus. The successful candidate will be required to undertake research activities at other Griffith University or Australian Red Cross Lifeblood locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. Salary Range \nThe successful candidate will be appointed at either Research Fellow Grade 1 or Research Fellow Grade 2 , depending on their qualifications, skills, experience and demonstrated level of research expertise. Base salary range for Research Fellow Grade 1 $91,705 - $107,420 per annum + 17% superannuation.  \nBase salary range for Research Fellow Grade 2 $117,076 - $139,031 per annum + 17% supernnauation. \n\n Why Griffith? \nAs a values-led organisation, at Griffith University, we've worked hard to create a dynamic and strong organisational culture. We offer:   Access to advanced glycomics, glycoproteomics, mass spectrometry, structural biology, cell-culture and blood-rheology capabilities. Mentorship from investigators with complementary expertise across molecular glycoscience, structural biology, erythropoiesis and red blood cell physiology. Close collaboration with Australian Red Cross Lifeblood and exposure to a nationally significant translational research environment. Opportunities to establish national and international collaborations. Support for high-quality publications, conference participation and professional development. Opportunities to contribute to research student supervision, grant development and future translational research. Griffith University employment benefits, including 17% employer superannuation, generous leave provisions, salary packaging and professional development opportunities. How to apply \nAt Griffith, we’re committed to providing a safe and inclusive environment for all - whoever you are and wherever you’re from. If you require any accommodations, we welcome you to let us know so we can work with you to participate fully in our recruitment experience. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. For more information please visit our Equity, Diversity and Inclusion page. Our University is committed to fostering a safe, respectful and inclusive community for all. In alignment with the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Respond to Gender-based Violence (2025), Griffith University includes a declaration as part of the recruitment journey to uphold the values and standards of our community. Griffith University also maintains a strict zero-tolerance policy against all forms of modern slavery. For more information, please refer to Griffith’s Modern Slavery Policy . Please submit your application online and ensure it includes the following: A covering letter outlining your suitability for the role (please refer to the qualifications above and the position description ) Current curriculum vitae/resume including full contact details Names and contact details of at least three professional referees (no contact will be made without your permission) Visa sponsorship may be available to the successful candidate. For further information specifically about this role, please contact Professor Daniel Kolarich, Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics at  d.kolarich@griffith.edu.au    Closing date:  11:59pm AEST 20 September 2026.  All applications must be submitted online. Please allow at least 1 – 2 weeks from the closing date to expect an application update. \n\n Every institution values excellence. What matters most is why. \nGriffith was created to be a different type of university. You’ll find we’re about leading research, academic excellence, and the transformative power of education. But what sets us apart is why those things matter and how you’ll achieve them. Why? Griffith brings together exceptional minds like yours from across the globe and from all walks of life. Here, we do incredible work, questioning and challenging, always in the pursuit of excellence.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144457779": {
      "title": "Global Safety Innovation PhD Scholarship: Improving the measurement and reporting of safety information within organisations",
      "description": "This PhD is grounded in real world practice. Global Safety Innovation (GSI) is a network of organisations across high-risk industries working toward safer workplaces. Rather than researching safety measurement from the outside, you will work directly with GSI's partner organisations throughout the PhD, spending focused blocks of time embedded within their operations to see firsthand how safety data and reporting systems are built, used, and contested. Success will mean working across two worlds: rigorous methodology with academic supervisors, and genuine collaboration with practitioners inside partner organisations. The project aims to improve understanding of the conditions under which innovation in safety measurement is possible, exploring questions such as: What factors increase or decrease the social acceptability of alternative measures within and beyond organisations? What makes a new measure practically useful for improving safety? And how is consensus around new measures established, maintained, and translated into practice? Your supervision team for this project brings together expertise in organisational safety, community-engaged research, and human factors. You will be supervised by Dr Clarissa Carden, an interdisciplinary researcher specialising in community safety, wellbeing, and social justice; Associate Professor Drew Rae, a leading safety researcher whose work examines how organisations manage risk and safety performance; and Zoë Nation, a human factors and safety specialist focused on improving the systems, practices, and behaviours that shape safety outcomes. This position is primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or Gold Coast. As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. \n\n The selection of applicants for the award of higher degree research scholarships at Griffith University involves consideration of your academic merit and research background. To be successful within this role, a background in sociology or safety science is desirable. You will have evidence of a passion for social research, with a strong desire to undertake practical, applied scholarship to respond to industry challenges. Ideally you will have had some experience in heavy industry or applying safety in a working context. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with honours equivalent to  second  class honours or a Masters degree (AQF Level 9) incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the  Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . International applicants to research programs must demonstrate they meet the program’s English Language Proficiency requirements, as outlined below: A minimum overall band score of 6.5 on the IELTS (Academic) with no band score less than 6.0 OR an overall score of 79 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with no sub-score less than 19 (applies to tests taken prior to 21 January 2026) OR an overall score of 4 on the internet-based (iBT) TOEFL with a minimum score of 4 in each of the listening, reading, and writing skills, and a minimum score of 3.5 in speaking skills (applies to tests taken on or after 21 January 2026) OR a score of no less than 3+ in each skill of the ISLPR (conducted by ISLPR Language Services only) OR a minimum overall score of 176 (no score less than 169) on C1 Advanced  or  C2 Proficiency OR an overall score of 58 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 50 (applies to tests taken prior to 7 August 2025) OR an overall score of 55 in the Pearson Test of English (Academic) with no score less than 47 (applies to tests taken on or after 7 August 2025) OR an overall score of 70 in the LanguageCert Academic OR an overall score of 59 in the Michigan English Test (MET). English test results must have been obtained within two years of the program commencement date, and only in-person test results will be accepted. \n\n About the scholarship \nThis scholarship will be jointly funded by Global Safety Innovation and Griffith University. The total scholarship value is $50,000 per annum for three years, with the possibility of a six-month extension. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study at a university that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in Southeast Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility, and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to ' clarissa.carden@griffith.edu.au ' , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Friday 11th of September 2026, at 11:59PM AEST. The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nDr Clarissa Carden \nEmail:  clarissa.carden@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact:  \nGriffith Graduate Research School  \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n The School of Humanities, Languages and Social Science proudly hosts world-class researchers in a diverse range of specialist fields. We are home to the Griffith Centre for Social and Cultural Research (GCSCR), which is dedicated to increasing our understanding of current challenges facing the modern world. Through the application of innovative humanities and social science research, incorporating the knowledge of academics from a broad range of interrelated disciplines, we are at the cutting edge of humanities and social science discourse. We are also home to the Safety Science Innovation Lab which was founded to capitalise on the opportunities for collaboration between science, health, business and the humanities and social sciences. Our approach recognises that critical innovations in safety science won't necessarily come from new technologies, but from a better understanding of complexity and social construction. The successful applicant will be based at either Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan) or the Gold Coast.  Remote study may be available, subject to approval. The project will require regular collaboration with Global Safety Innovation Summit (GSI), including focused blocks of time throughout the PhD embedded within GSI or participating companies. The supervisors for this project will be Dr Clarissa Carden, Associate Professor Drew Rae, and Zoe Nation, Director of Global Safety Innovation.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144456009": {
      "error": "not present in bulk fetch result"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000144455125": {
      "title": "PhD scholarship: Mode of Action Studies of Marine Bacillus Probiotics Using Multi-omics Approaches",
      "description": "We are currently looking for a PhD candidate to undertake research on the mode of action of marine  Bacillus  probiotics using advanced multi-omics approaches. This project forms part of a larger collaborative research initiative between Griffith University, Bioproton Pty Ltd and the Marine Bioproducts Cooperative Research Centre (MBCRC). The project provides an opportunity to develop high-level skills in microbiology, probiotic research, metabolomics, microbiome analysis, bioinformatics and systems biology, while working in a collaborative academic–industry environment. The successful candidate will engage with industry partners and MBCRC to gain insight into product development, regulatory requirements and commercial translation, and will be supported to disseminate research outcomes through publications, presentations and collaborative activities relevant to Australia’s growing marine bioproducts sector. This position is primarily based at Griffith University Brisbane South (Nathan). As Griffith is a multi-campus University you may be required to work across other campus locations. Griffith University’s sites are located on the lands of the Yugarabul, Yuggera, Jagera, Turrbal, Yugambeh and Kombumerri peoples. \n\n The successful applicant should have a strong academic background in microbiology, biotechnology, biochemistry, molecular biology, bioinformatics, or a related discipline. You will have evidence of curiosity, initiative and a strong interest in applying advanced research methods to understand host–microbe interactions and the mechanisms underpinning probiotic efficacy. The successful applicant will be expected to work both independently and as part of a collaborative research team, communicate effectively with academic and industry partners, and contribute to publications, presentations and project reporting. Expressions of interest are welcome from domestic and onshore international applicants and international applicants. If you are an international applicant who is currently offshore please consider the viability of travelling costs before submitting an expression of interest. Please see the  FAQs  web page for requirements for offshore international applicants. Applicants must have completed, or expect to complete, a bachelors degree with First Class Honours (H1) or equivalent, or a master's degree (AQF Level 9) with a substantial research component demonstrating academic excellence. Honours Class 2A or equivalent are encouraged to apply. Masters degree incorporating a significant research component of a standard comparable to a bachelor honours degree or be regarded by Griffith University as having an equivalent level of attainment in accordance with the  Equivalent qualifications for HDR program admission . For further information on the eligibility requirements for the program refer  here . Applicants are advised that the following skills are highly desirable: Strong analytical and problem-solving, with the ability to design and conduct independent research. Experience with microbiome analysis, next-generation sequencing, bioinformatics, molecular microbiology, cell culture, immunological assays, metabolomics or systems biology approaches. Experience working in industry, clinical, translational or collaborative research environments. Evidence of competitive scholarships, academic awards or research excellence prizes. Programming and data analysis skills \n\n About the scholarship \nThis scholarship is a MBCRC PhD scholarship providing a stipend of AUD $60,000 per annum for a period of up to 3.5 years, subject to satisfactory progress and continued enrolment in the doctoral program. The scholarship is intended to support a full-time PhD candidate undertaking this industry-linked research project. The scholarship will be awarded in accordance with Griffith University’s higher degree research scholarship conditions and any applicable project funding requirements. What Griffith offers \nAs a scholarship holder, you will study with Griffith University that ranks among the top 2% worldwide and spans across campuses in South East Queensland. At Griffith University, we’ve worked hard to create a culture that will challenge you to be curious, creative and courageous. We also support the professional and personal development of all our HDR candidates and invest in the skills of our people. Griffith University values diversity, inclusion and flexibility and we encourage Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander, and people of all backgrounds to apply. Griffith's strategic goals are to also increase the proportion of women in senior academic and administrative roles and in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics and Medicine (STEMM). At the Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics, you will join a collaborative and multidisciplinary research environment with access to experienced supervisors, advanced laboratory facilities and expertise across microbiology, natural product chemistry, bioanalysis, glycomics, metabolomics and translational biomedical research. The Institute provides a supportive setting for HDR candidates to develop as independent researchers while engaging with interdisciplinary teams and external partners. Through this project, you will be supported to build technical and analytical capability in multi-omics approaches, microbiome and metabolomic data interpretation, probiotic mechanism-of-action studies and industry-relevant research translation. The project will also provide opportunities to interact with Bioproton Pty Ltd and MBCRC, enabling you to gain practical insight into industry-led research priorities, product development pathways, regulatory considerations and commercial translation. You will also have opportunities to contribute to publications, present findings to academic and industry audiences, and gain experience working within a research program connected to Australia’s emerging marine bioproducts sector. EOI Process \nPlease do  not  select the “APPLY” button. All expressions of interest must be submitted to Associate Professor Yun Feng at  ' y.feng@griffith.edu.au ' , containing: Statement addressing your suitability for the project/scholarship Evidence that you have completed a program with the required grades as detailed in the ‘About you’ section A curriculum vitae (CV) Names and contact details of two referees Closing date for expressions of interest is:  Friday 18 September 2026 5pm AEST The preferred applicant will then be invited to apply for the program and scholarship on-line For project related enquiries please contact: \nAssociate Professor Yun Feng \nEmail:  y.feng@griffith.edu.au For administration enquiries please contact: \nGriffith Graduate Research School \nEmail:  hdr-scholarship@griffith.edu.au \n\n This scholarship will be based within Griffith University’s Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics, a multidisciplinary biomedical research institute that brings together expertise in discovery and translational research, advanced infrastructure and research training. Candidates will join a collaborative and well-equipped research environment that supports innovation from fundamental discovery through to applied and translational outcomes. The successful applicant will be based at Griffith University’s Brisbane South (Nathan) campus. The Principal Supervisor for this project, Associate Professor Yun Feng, is a Research Leader at Griffith University’s Institute for Biomedicine and Glycomics whose research focuses on natural products, marine microbiology, and biotechnology. Her work uses bioactive compounds from nature to advance treatments for neurological diseases and develop innovative solutions for animal health, food safety, and agriculture.",
      "department": "DVC Research"
    },
    "https://jobs.smartrecruiters.com/GriffithUniversity/744000143740949": {
      "error": "not present in bulk fetch result"
